---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ReviewedByCard from "../../components/ReviewedByCard.astro";
import { TRUST_PROFILES } from "../../config/trust";

const title = "Down Payment Impact on Mortgage Payments";
const description =
  "How down payment size changes monthly mortgage payments, PMI, and total cost. Educational use only.";

const lastUpdated = "2026-04-05";
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/guides/mortgage-payment-down-payment-impact"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  authorProfile={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
  reviewProfiles={[TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview, TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview]}
  robots="noindex, follow"
>
  <section class="card hero">
    <span class="pill">Guide</span>
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/guides">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Down payment impact</span>
    </nav>
    <h1>Down payment impact on mortgage payments</h1>
    <p>
      A larger down payment reduces the loan balance and can remove PMI, but it also uses more cash. This guide shows how to model
      the payment impact and tradeoffs.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Mortgage payment calculator</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/estimating-pmi-cost">Estimating PMI cost</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/mortgage-payment-affordability-checklist">Affordability checklist</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <ReviewedByCard
    writtenBy={TRUST_PROFILES.siteOwner}
    reviewedBy={TRUST_PROFILES.methodologyReview}
    secondaryReview={TRUST_PROFILES.editorialReview}
    reviewScope="Down-payment-versus-reserves framing, PMI threshold and cash-to-close interpretation, and routing between mortgage payment, PMI, and affordability workflows."
    reviewedOn="2026-04-05"
  />

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Use this guide when the main tradeoff is down payment versus reserves and PMI</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Use this page when the question is not just how to lower the payment, but whether using more cash upfront is worth the lower loan balance or lower PMI.</li>
      <li>If you need the general payment formula first, move to <a href="/guides/how-mortgage-payments-are-calculated">how mortgage payments are calculated</a>.</li>
      <li>If PMI is the main variable driving the comparison, move next to <a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-pmi-thresholds">PMI thresholds and mortgage payments</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Mortgage payment inputs to verify</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Loan amount, note rate, and term.</li>
      <li>Taxes, insurance, HOA, and PMI assumptions.</li>
      <li>Escrow rules or reserves used by the lender.</li>
      <li>Time horizon if comparing scenarios.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">What changes with a bigger down payment</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Lower loan balance and monthly principal &amp; interest.</li>
      <li>Potentially lower PMI or no PMI at 20%+ down.</li>
      <li>Less cash available for reserves or investments.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Scenario template</h2>
    <ol class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Run 5%, 10%, and 20% down payment scenarios.</li>
      <li>Compare the monthly payment and PMI impact.</li>
      <li>Compare total interest over your expected horizon.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Ignoring cash reserves and emergency funds.</li>
      <li>Assuming PMI is the same across all down payments.</li>
      <li>Only comparing monthly payment and skipping total cost.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Cash-to-close checklist</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Down payment amount for each scenario.</li>
      <li>Estimated closing costs and prepaids.</li>
      <li>Target cash reserves after closing.</li>
      <li>Planned repairs or moving expenses.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Decision inputs</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Target reserve amount you want after closing.</li>
      <li>PMI removal threshold you aim to reach.</li>
      <li>Expected ownership horizon and refinance plans.</li>
      <li>Rate quotes at different down payment levels.</li>
      <li>Opportunity cost assumptions for the extra cash.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Break-even check</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Estimate how long it takes to offset the extra cash outlay.</li>
      <li>Compare PMI savings vs higher cash reserves.</li>
      <li>Consider investment alternatives for the cash difference.</li>
      <li>Match the analysis to your planned ownership horizon.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Why this is a cash-management decision</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      A larger down payment can improve the monthly payment, but it can also leave you with less cushion for closing costs, repairs,
      income shocks, or investment alternatives. That is why this comparison belongs beside affordability, PMI, and reserve planning rather than payment math alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Scenario checklist</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>Compare 5%, 10%, and 20% down side by side.</li>
      <li>Include PMI, taxes, insurance, and HOA in each scenario.</li>
      <li>Use the same rate and term for a clean comparison.</li>
      <li>Track cash-to-close and remaining reserves.</li>
      <li>Re-run if rates or home price change.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="grid" style="margin-top:18px">
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Related tools</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li><a href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Mortgage payment calculator</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card section prose col-6">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">Related guides</h2>
      <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
        <li><a href="/guides/how-mortgage-payments-are-calculated">Mortgage payment math</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-pmi-thresholds">PMI thresholds</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/mortgage-payment-prepaids-and-reserves">Prepaids and reserves</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/estimating-pmi-cost">Estimating PMI cost</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/pmi-removal-vs-extra-principal">PMI removal vs extra principal</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/what-is-piti">What is PITI?</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">References</h2>
    <ul class="muted" style="margin:10px 0 0; padding-left:18px">
      <li>
        CFPB:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/mortgages/ready-to-buy-a-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Ready to buy a home?
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        CFPB:{" "}
        <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/owning-a-home/explore/home-loan-toolkit/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Your home loan toolkit
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card section prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Next steps</h2>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:10px">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/calculators/mortgage-payment-calculator">Run the payment calculator</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/estimating-pmi-cost">Estimate PMI cost</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/guides/mortgage-payment-affordability-checklist">Check affordability</a>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0">Educational use only. Not financial advice.</p>
    <p class="muted" style="margin:12px 0 0; font-size:13px">Last updated: 2026-04-05</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>



